    <version>2.0.17</version>
    <time>1783525037</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Update to 2.0.17: clamp work-group size to per-kernel device max; fix DCE-inflated throughput in CUDA WMMA/MMA and Metal simdgroup_matrix benchmarks; native mma.sync tensor-core path with full-rate FP16; joint_matrix on Intel Arc A770.</comment>
    <requestid>1364433</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="15" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>b9c00bcf77db23d4a1edbbc286ed31d4</srcmd5>
    <version>2.0.18</version>
    <time>1783945668</time>
    <user>dimstar_suse</user>
    <comment>- Update to 2.0.18:
  * Major expansion of the native CPU backend with new benchmark probes for
    recent x86 and ARM ISA extensions (AVX-VNNI / AVX10.2 / AMX, SVE/SVE2,
    AES/SHA crypto, string and UTF-8 validation kernels)</comment>
    <requestid>1365244</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="16" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>20a64bfe9e7b94cd14ee5941159bcd24</srcmd5>
    <version>2.0.19</version>
    <time>1786978748</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Update clpeak to 2.0.19.

Upstream fixes:
- OpenCL: the fp32/fp64/fp16 compute kernels now use a contracted
  (y*x)+y expression instead of the mad() builtin, which the spec only
  permits (not requires) to fuse into a hardware FMA; Apple's
  CL-on-Metal compiler never lowered it and halved the reported fp32
  throughput. Other platforms already fused it and are unaffected.
- CUDA/ROCm: the global-bandwidth kernel in/out pointers are marked
  __restrict__ so loads can be batched ahead of the trailing store.
- CPU backend: fixes the GCC build failure on the AArch64 crypto ISA
  extensions (upstream issue #189) -- GCC exposes the AES/SHA-256 NEON
  intrinsics only under the umbrella +crypto target attribute, so a TU
  built with just +aes or +sha2 failed to inline them. GCC now gets
  +crypto (+crypto+sha3 for the SHA-512 unit); clang keeps the granular
  flags.

Packaging:
- License corrected to Apache-2.0. Upstream relicensed away from the
  Unlicense in December 2023 (commit 65e57245) and the bundled LICENSE
  has carried the Apache License 2.0 ever since, but the spec still
  declared LicenseRef-SUSE-Public-Domain. LICENSE is the only licence
  file in the tree, there is no bundled third-party code and no source
  file carries a divergent SPDX header. Fedora declares Apache-2.0 too.
  A Legal-Review-Notice comment above the tag records the evidence.

No patches are carried by this package.

Verified: local osc build against openSUSE_Factory/aarch64 green,
%check 1/1 ctest passed, rpmlint 0 errors / 0 badness, source_validator
clean.</comment>
    <requestid>1371494</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="17" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>52a9e1582ecf1d344da9b309b454fca5</srcmd5>
    <version>2.1.1</version>
    <time>1787155147</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>- Update to 2.1.1:
  * New CLI tests: bilinear texture sample rate
    (--texture-sample), store-to-load forwarding latency
    (--store-forward), and SMT scaling of fp32 throughput
    (--smt-scaling).
  * Rebuild compute MAD chains as a single accumulator across
    all backends; the old two-register ping-pong understated
    fp32 peak on some GPUs. Numbers on the same machine can be
    higher than 2.0.19.
  * Fix CPU cache-bandwidth measurement (read/write/copy) and
    stop the AVX-512 FP16 chain collapsing under fast-math.
  * --describe prints what each test and reading measures.
  * Overlapping TestScopes no longer silently drop result rows
    from the text output.
  * Exclude per-ISA CPU translation units from LTO so distro
    -flto builds keep their -m/-march flags at link time.
  * 2.1.1: drop the AMX-TF32 matrix test (Intel removed the ISA
    before any silicon shipped) and probe AMX tile ops at
    configure time instead of trusting compiler flags
    (gh#krrishnarraj/clpeak#193, gh#krrishnarraj/clpeak#195).
  * Upstream also adds a Flutter desktop/mobile GUI; it is not
    packaged here (no Flutter SDK in Factory).
- Enable the Vulkan backend (pkgconfig(vulkan), vulkan-headers,
  shaderc) and drop the unused pkgconfig(gl) BuildRequires.
- Set the tarball version fallback so clpeak --version reports
  2.1.1 rather than the stale 2.0.16
  (gh#krrishnarraj/clpeak#198).
</comment>
    <requestid>1371923</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="18" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>7fca8f0a3416dd15140237113047c919</srcmd5>
    <version>2.1.2</version>
    <time>1787324180</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Thu Aug 20 18:40:38 UTC 2026 - Martin Pluskal &lt;mpluskal@suse.com&gt;

- Update to 2.1.2:
  * Keep machine-readable output locale-independent so XML
    dumps, JSON/CSV exports and parsed values always use '.'
    as the decimal separator, including under comma-decimal
    locales (gh#krrishnarraj/clpeak#199).</comment>
    <requestid>1372634</requestid>
  </revision>
  <revision rev="19" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>6131113ffed236ad8bb6ac5bf8e65828</srcmd5>
    <version>2.1.3</version>
    <time>1787656776</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Update to 2.1.3.

Upstream release of 2026-08-24. Mostly measurement correctness: four
Intel Arc faults fixed (Vulkan device features were requested with their
extensions enabled but every feature flag left VK_FALSE, and
cooperative-matrix stages are now pinned to a 32-wide subgroup so a
driver splitting the group stops dividing the reported rate), a second
MAD-chain shape raced per compute family after the shared squaring chain
was found to report exactly half of Alchemist/Xe-HPG throughput, and
corrections to CUDA int8 sparsity and to OpenCL mixed-precision, which
read 8x high on work-item-vectorising CPU runtimes. New CUDA FP8 rows
complete the dense/sparse grid.

Packaging: 2.1.3 removes the hardcoded in-tree version fallback, so a
tarball build (no .git, no git-describe) now reports &quot;unknown&quot; in
--version and in the clpeak_version field of every JSON, XML and CSV
export. The %prep sed we carried for that variable became a silent
no-op, so it is dropped and replaced by clpeak-version-override.patch,
which adds -DCLPEAK_VERSION_OVERRIDE. Our source is exactly the tag, so
the number is a fact we hold and git does not. Sent upstream as
gh#krrishnarraj/clpeak#206; drop the patch when it is released.

Verified by running the built binary in the build chroot: it prints
&quot;clpeak version: 2.1.3&quot;. Built on aarch64 against openSUSE_Factory,
ctest 1/1 passed, rpmlint 0 errors 0 warnings 0 badness.</comment>
    <requestid>1373454</requestid>
  </revision>
